it's a health workers nightmare a busy  border crossing thousands of people in  close contact and constantly on the move  this medical worker tells people to wash  their hands health checks and hygiene  measures have tightened between the  Democratic Republic of Congo and Rwanda  ever since multiple cases of the Ebola  virus were discovered here in Goma the  Congolese city of more than two million  people people are washing their hands as  a precaution due to the outbreak of  disease the telus that is spreading  through the hands that's why they are  asking us to wash with water mixed with  medication the virus is passed through  bodily fluids including sweat there is a  vaccine that has been used for quite  some time the World Health Organization  says it has a very high success rate and  you only need one shot but there's a  problem the vaccination is not approved  for mass use it's usually given to those  exposed to the virus and it's also given  to the doctors and health workers as  they are handling those cases to protect  them from contracting the virus there is  still no officially approved Ebola  vaccine on the market but we're told  this one is expected to be officially  approved at some stage there is also a  lot of confusion around the virus and a  lot of suspicion of foreign health  workers here in eastern DRC it's a  remote region that has been neglected by  the government and torn apart by years  of conflict it would have been better if  our leaders and those who are concerned  would give us more information they have  not informed us where we can get this  vaccine so that all of us can go and  actually get it but for sure all of us  need to be vaccinated this is the second  worst Ebola outbreak on record more than  a thousand 800 people have died of the  virus in the DRC over the past year but  the fact that it has now reached a  border city is what's causing renewed  alarm it prompted the government here in  Rwanda to close its western border for a  few hours on Thursday as it beefed up  its health screening procedures are the  problem with this virus is once you  identify someone infected by it do they  need to trace back all the people they  have come in  contact with test them vaccinate them  and potentially quarantine them it is a  highly infectious disease and containing  it is a real challenge Stephanie Decker  al Jazeera Kigali
 

